    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1082">

                <pb facs="1126" n="1114" sameAs="frus:frus1945v08/pg1114"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890D.01/5–2945: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister to Syria and Lebanon</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wadsworth</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Beirut</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-05-29T21:00:00+02:00">May 29, 1945—9
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 293:54 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>163. Supplementing my 162 of May 29:<anchor n="63" xml:id="d1082-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>At 7:30 p.m. Porter<anchor n="64" xml:id="d1082-f2"/> phoned me from the Orient
                    Palace Hotel Damascus reporting heavy machine gun and rifle fire from direction
                    French barracks between hotel and bazaar.</p>
                <p>As we were talking, answering fire came from various nearby places including roof
                    of hotel; then reported [<hi rendition="#italic">repeated?</hi>] machine gun
                    bursts (which I heard over telephone) came from neighboring French Foyer des
                    Soldats; then line was cut.</p>
                <p>Ten minutes later BGS<anchor n="65" xml:id="d1082-f3"/> Ninth Army telephoned me
                    “French are <pb facs="1127" n="1115" xml:id="pg1115"/> shelling Damascus;
                    General Pilleau will see General Humblot<anchor n="66" xml:id="d1082-f4"/> at
                    once; I am off for Damascus; can’t dine with you; good-by”.</p>
                <p>At 8 p.m. Satterthwaite phoned from Brit Leg that French airplane had just
                    dropped bombs on and machine-gunned city.</p>
                <p>Rptd Paris as 55; sent Dept as 163; paraphrases to Arab cap[ital]s.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wadsworth</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1082-f1" n="63" xml:id="d1082-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported that Damascus was “seething” and that the
                    “conflagration” in Syria was spreading, notably in the Jebel Druze where all
                    Frenchmen were prisoners of the Syrian Governor and several hundred <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Troupes Spéciales</hi> had transferred their allegiance
                    to the Governor (890E.01/5–2945).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1082-f2" n="64" xml:id="d1082-f2-content">
                <p>William J. Porter, Vice Consul and Attaché at Damascus.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1082-f3" n="65" xml:id="d1082-f3-content">
                <p>Brigadier General Staff (Jasper G. Frere).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1082-f4" n="66" xml:id="d1082-f4-content">
                <p>Gen. Emile Humblot, Commander of French forces in Syria and Lebanon.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
